<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<sbgn xmlns="http://sbgn.org/libsbgn/0.3" xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0">
  <map id="QBM-02" language="process description">
    <extension>
      <qbo:mapNotes xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0">Photosynthetic electron transport and plastid redox. The photosynthetic chain redrawn with each node's quantum chemistry and the evidence behind it. Photosystem I is the strongest structural case in the atlas: charge separation at P700 makes a genuine spin-correlated radical pair, the species the radical-pair mechanism was written for. It is nevertheless only T2, because the one direct field measurement on photosynthesis in the review's bibliography was made in lima bean rather than Arabidopsis, and because that pair is extremely short-lived and its known field effects are seen well above geomagnetic strength. Cyclic electron flow is drawn for the same reason alternative oxidase appears on QBM-01 — it lets a chloroplast rebalance ATP against NADPH without changing linear flux, so measuring linear electron transport alone cannot separate "no effect" from "compensated effect". Four nodes are plastid-encoded (ATCG loci) and will receive no value from poly-A-selected RNA-seq, which is most of OSDR's plant studies. Derived from: Figures 1B and 5 of Porterfield &amp; Barker, "Plant Responses to Near-Null Magnetic Fields" — expanded to gene level and bound to Arabidopsis loci</qbo:mapNotes>
    </extension>
    <glyph id="thylakoid_lumen" class="compartment">
      <label text="Thylakoid lumen" />
      <bbox x="172.78" y="-26.00" w="330.99" h="99.44" />
    </glyph>
