    <glyph id="PLASTOCYANIN" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="thylakoid_lumen">
      <label text="Plastocyanin" />
      <bbox x="328.78" y="0.00" w="148.99" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="PLASTOCYANIN__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="type-I Cu" />
        <bbox x="334.78" y="-9.00" w="63.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:PLASTOCYANIN" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>spin_relaxation_modulated</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT1G76100</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT1G20340</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0009543</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>A mononuclear Cu(II)/Cu(I) centre performing one-electron transfer, so formally paramagnetic in the oxidised state.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>Deliberately classed as spin_relaxation_modulated rather than as a radical-pair partner. A single copper centre relaxes fast and has no correlated partner, so coherent singlet-triplet mixing is not available here; only a relaxation-based effect could be argued, and none has been shown.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="PSII" class="complex" compartmentRef="thylakoid_membrane">
      <label text="Photosystem II" />
      <bbox x="84.75" y="137.44" w="149.02" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="PSII__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="Mn4CaO5" />
        <bbox x="90.75" y="128.44" w="49.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <glyph id="PSII__u1" class="unit of information">
        <label text="QA / QB" />
        <bbox x="90.75" y="128.44" w="49.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:PSII" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>semiquinone_radical</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>ros_leak_site</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">ATCG00020</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT1G06680</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0009523</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">1.10.3.9</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>The QA/QB sites hold a kinetically stabilised plastosemiquinone, and PSII is a major singlet-oxygen source under excess light. Both are one-electron chemistry in a defined pocket, which is the setting a radical-pair argument needs.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>PSII's dominant ROS species is singlet oxygen from the P680 triplet, not superoxide. Triplet chlorophyll formation is itself spin chemistry, so a field effect here would be more plausible than at most nodes — but it is also a route the review does not develop, and no measurement exists.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="PLASTOQUINONE_POOL" class="simple chemical" compartmentRef="thylakoid_membrane">
      <label text="Plastoquinone pool" />
      <bbox x="267.77" y="143.84" w="145.18" h="34.64" />
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:PLASTOQUINONE_POOL" evidenceTier="T4" confidence="verified">
          <qbo:quantumClass>structural_context</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:xref db="chebi">CHEBI:28377</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="kegg">C02061</qbo:xref>
